# Oracle Price Discovery and Exchange Reference Prices

## Overview

This document explains how oracle-based price data and exchange-based price data can describe the same crypto asset while being produced through different mechanisms.

The project focuses on BTC/USD, ETH/USD, and SOL/USD price-source comparison between Binance spot market data and Chainlink Data Streams.

## Exchange-Traded Prices

Binance spot prices are exchange-traded prices.

They are formed through:

- Limit orders
- Market orders
- Order book liquidity
- Taker and maker activity
- Spread
- Short-term volatility
- Real transaction flow

Because exchange prices come from active trading, they can react quickly to market information.

## Oracle-Reported Prices

Chainlink Data Streams prices are oracle-reported prices.

They are not formed by a single exchange order book. Instead, they are based on external market data sources and Chainlink's reporting infrastructure.

Oracle prices may differ from a single exchange price because of:

- Data aggregation methodology
- Reporting timestamp
- Reference market composition
- Bid/ask construction
- Latency
- Data validation rules

## Why Binance and Chainlink May Differ

Binance and Chainlink can both reference BTC/USD, ETH/USD, or SOL/USD, but they do not necessarily produce identical values at every timestamp.

Differences may appear because:

- Binance reflects one trading venue
- Chainlink reflects oracle-reported market data
- Timestamps may not perfectly match
- Short-term volatility can create temporary gaps
- Data sources may update at different moments
- Reference prices and traded prices are not always identical

